    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(4))  # F0..F3\n    customers = list(range(11))  # C0..C10\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(52, 93), (50, 24), (94, 94), (8, 44)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(53, 59), (66, 32), (54, 78), (80, 66), (53, 78), (71, 28), (26, 10), (30, 74), (29, 41), (27, 43), (81, 53)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [240.0, 173.0, 158.0, 192.0]\n    customer_demands = [18.0, 11.0, 37.0, 43.0, 43.0, 36.0, 39.0, 36.0, 48.0, 22.0, 27.0]\n    fixed_costs = [301.0, 171.0, 336.0, 261.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.92  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Warehouse network decision \u2014 which sites to open and how to serve customers\n\nWe need to decide which warehouses to open and how to route shipments so we meet all customer demand at the lowest overall cost. Our team has compiled the relevant inputs: candidate warehouse locations and customer sites on the map, the cost to open each warehouse, the shipping rate, and the capacity and demand figures we have to work within. The facility locations are provided in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (52, 93)\n  - F1: (50, 24)\n  - F2: (94, 94)\n  - F3: (8, 44), and the customer locations are provided in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (53, 59)\n  - C1: (66, 32)\n  - C2: (54, 78)\n  - C3: (80, 66)\n  - C4: (53, 78)\n  - C5: (71, 28)\n  - C6: (26, 10)\n  - C7: (30, 74)\n  - C8: (29, 41)\n  - C9: (27, 43)\n  - C10: (81, 53). Opening any warehouse entails an upfront cost shown in [301.0, 171.0, 336.0, 261.0]. Each warehouse can only handle up to its capacity, as listed in [240.0, 173.0, 158.0, 192.0], and each customer has demand that must be fully served, as shown in [18.0, 11.0, 37.0, 43.0, 43.0, 36.0, 39.0, 36.0, 48.0, 22.0, 27.0]. Shipping costs vary based on how far the product travels and how much we ship, at the rate specified in 0.92.\n\nPractically, this is a trade-off between the fixed cost of opening sites and the variable cost of serving customers from those sites. Opening more warehouses can reduce delivery distances and potentially lower shipping costs, but it increases fixed costs and may leave capacity underutilized. Opening fewer warehouses keeps fixed costs down, but it can drive up shipping costs if the remaining sites are far from certain customer clusters or if their capacities become bottlenecks.\n\nKey rules we must uphold:\n- A warehouse can only ship if we choose to open it.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied.\n- No warehouse can exceed its capacity.\n- Shipping costs depend on distance and the volume shipped.\n\nOur objective is to minimize total cost \u2014 the combination of warehouse opening costs and shipping costs \u2014 while respecting these constraints. The recommended outcome will specify which warehouses to open and how to allocate each customer\u2019s demand across the open sites, balancing proximity, capacity, and cost so we achieve the most economical network configuration.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Warehouse network decision \u2014 which sites to open and how to serve our customers\n\nTeam,\n\nWe need to decide which warehouses to open from our shortlist and how to route orders to our customers so that we meet demand at the lowest possible total cost.\n\nWhat we know:\n- We have a set of candidate warehouse locations across our service region, provided as map coordinates in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}. Our customer locations, also given as map coordinates, are listed in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}.\n- Opening a warehouse carries an upfront expense. Those location-by-location amounts are summarized in {FIXED_COSTS}.\n- Each site can handle only so much throughput. Those limits are outlined in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}.\n- Every customer has orders that must be fully satisfied within the planning window. Those requirements are shown in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}.\n- Shipping spend depends on how far we move product and how much we move. Our variable transport rate is {TRANSPORT_RATE}, so longer hauls and higher volumes will cost more.\n\nKey business rules:\n- A facility can only ship if we choose to open it.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be covered in full; we can\u2019t leave orders unserved.\n- No warehouse can be assigned more volume than its capacity.\n\nThe decision in front of us is a balance of trade-offs. Opening more sites can shorten average delivery distances and reduce transportation spend, but increases our fixed costs. Opening fewer sites saves on fixed costs, but may drive up shipping and risk hitting capacity ceilings. Our goal is to find the combination of warehouses to open and the shipment plan from those sites to each customer that minimizes total cost\u2014both the cost to open facilities and the cost to move product\u2014while reliably meeting demand.\n\nDeliverable: a recommended set of warehouses to open and a shipment plan that shows how customer demand will be served from the open network, with the expected total cost and a brief rationale for the choice."
